{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לא תסיג גבול", :translit "lo tasig gevul", :gloss "you shall not move a boundary"} {:he "רעך", :translit "re'ekha", :gloss "your neighbor"} {:he "לרוצח", :translit "larotze'ach", :gloss "to the murderer"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah: the prohibition of boundary-moving is written adjacent to laws of the murderer, motivating the a fortiori argument."}], :frame {:text "The section addressing murderers precedes the prohibition of boundary-moving in Deuteronomy 19. The ba'al ha-turim constructs a kal va-chomer: if the murderer's body may be taken (Numbers 35:16-21), surely his property may be appropriated.", :sources ["Numbers 35:16-21" "Deuteronomy 19:11-13" "Deuteronomy 19:14"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מישראל וטוב לך", :translit "mi-yisra'el ve-tov lakh", :gloss "from Israel and good for you"} {:he "לא תסיג", :translit "lo tasig", :gloss "you shall not move"} {:he "בעון מסיג גבול", :translit "be-avon masig gevul", :gloss "on account of the sin of moving a boundary"} {:he "נחרב הבית", :translit "neherav ha-bayit", :gloss "the House was destroyed"} {:he "הוי מגיעי בית בבית", :translit "hoy magi'i bayit ba-vayit", :gloss "woe to those who add house to house"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah and historical consequence: the phrase 'mi-yisra'el ve-tov lakh' from verse 6 is adjacent to 'lo tasig gevul' in verse 14, linking boundary-moving to destruction."}], :frame {:text "Isaiah 5:8 condemns those who extend their holdings house by house, which the Talmud identifies as an act of boundary violation. Makkot 5b states that because of the sin of moving boundaries the Temple was destroyed, as it is written 'Woe to those who add house to house' (Isaiah 5:8).", :sources ["Makkot 5b" "Isaiah 5:8"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לא תסיג גבול", :translit "lo tasig gevul", :gloss "you shall not move a boundary"} {:he "אזהרה לב\"ד", :translit "azharah le-veit din", :gloss "a warning to the court"} {:he "שלא יחליפו", :translit "she-lo yachlifu", :gloss "that they not exchange"} {:he "אחת מערי מקלט", :translit "achat me-arei miklat", :gloss "any one of the cities of refuge"} {:he "בעיר אחרת", :translit "be-ir acheret", :gloss "for another city"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Midrashic extension: the verse's literal prohibition of land theft is read as a directive to courts governing the cities of refuge."}], :frame {:text "Makkot 9b-10a records that the court may not exchange one city of refuge for another, since all cities of refuge must remain designated as such. The Talmud derives that just as a private individual may not move a boundary marker, so the court may not alter the territorial designation of a city of refuge.", :sources ["Makkot 9b" "Makkot 10a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לרשתה", :translit "la-reshetah", :gloss "to possess it"} {:he "לא יקום עד אחד באיש", :translit "lo yakum ed echad be-ish", :gloss "a single witness shall not stand against a person"} {:he "עד כאן", :translit "ad kan", :gloss "up to here"} {:he "תחומך", :translit "tekhumekha", :gloss "your boundary"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah: 'lo yakum ed echad be-ish' (v. 15) follows the verse about boundary-moving, linking the two topics."}], :frame {:text "Makkot 5b interprets the phrase 'be-khol avon u-ve-khol chet' in Deuteronomy 19:19 as encompassing both monetary cases and capital cases. The Talmud questions why a single witness cannot testify in monetary matters, since the verse mentions both 'iniquity' (monetary) and 'sin' (capital). The ba'al ha-turim notes that the single-witness prohibition (Deuteronomy 19:15) is juxtaposed with the boundary-moving verse.", :sources ["Makkot 5b" "Deuteronomy 19:15" "Deuteronomy 19:19"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לכל עון", :translit "le-khol avon", :gloss "for any iniquity"} {:he "היינו נפשות", :translit "hainu nafashot", :gloss "this is lives"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Lexical identification: the word 'avon' in this context refers to capital cases."}], :frame {:text "Makkot 5b interprets 'be-khol avon' as referring to capital cases (neshamot) and 'be-khol chet' as referring to monetary cases, based on the language used in Leviticus 4 regarding sin-offerings.", :sources ["Makkot 5b" "Leviticus 4"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לכל חטאת", :translit "le-khol chatat", :gloss "for any sin-offering"} {:he "היינו קרבן", :translit "hainu korban", :gloss "this is a sacrifice"} {:he "על פי שנים עדים", :translit "al pi shnayim edim", :gloss "on the testimony of two witnesses"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Lexical identification: 'chatat' here refers to a sacrifice, contrasted with 'avon' referring to capital cases."}], :frame {:text "Sanhedrin 29b describes how courts would intimidate witnesses with harsh language in capital cases but not in monetary cases, based on the severity of the outcome. Makkot 6a likewise discusses the difference in examination of witnesses between capital and monetary cases. The ba'al ha-turim links this procedural difference to the distinction between 'avon' (capital) and 'chatat' (monetary) within the same verse.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 29b" "Makkot 6a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah: the prohibition of boundary-moving is written adjacent to laws of the murderer, motivating the a fortiori argument.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhah and historical consequence: the phrase 'mi-yisra'el ve-tov lakh' from verse 6 is adjacent to 'lo tasig gevul' in verse 14, linking boundary-moving to destruction.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Midrashic extension: the verse's literal prohibition of land theft is read as a directive to courts governing the cities of refuge.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhah: 'lo yakum ed echad be-ish' (v. 15) follows the verse about boundary-moving, linking the two topics.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Lexical identification: the word 'avon' in this context refers to capital cases.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Lexical identification: 'chatat' here refers to a sacrifice, contrasted with 'avon' referring to capital cases.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:37:46.875684623Z", :model "glm-5.2"}